WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.767
The business question here is: list all patients and show Glucose tests when they exist.

00:00:06.767 --> 00:00:11.537
The words all patients are the most important part of the requirement.

00:00:11.537 --> 00:00:16.127
They tell us that the patient population must remain complete.

00:00:16.127 --> 00:00:20.172
Run the query with the Glucose condition inside ON.

00:00:20.172 --> 00:00:22.337
You should see every patient.

00:00:22.337 --> 00:00:25.907
Patients with Glucose tests will have lab values.

00:00:25.907 --> 00:00:31.052
Patients without a Glucose test will remain with NULL lab fields.

00:00:31.052 --> 00:00:34.897
Now imagine moving the same condition into WHERE.

00:00:34.897 --> 00:00:40.767
The result becomes a different population: only patients with Glucose tests.

00:00:40.767 --> 00:00:43.542
This is not just a syntax difference.

00:00:43.542 --> 00:00:46.492
It changes the meaning of the analysis.

00:00:46.492 --> 00:00:49.752
The same idea appears in many projects.

00:00:49.752 --> 00:00:56.112
A retailer may want every product and its sales from a specific month when sales exist.

00:00:56.112 --> 00:01:02.757
A university may want every student and a tutoring visit from this semester when one exists.

00:01:02.757 --> 00:01:08.767
If the optional condition belongs to the matched data but the full population must remain,

00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:11.467
ON is often the correct place.

00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:17.242
Try to explain the difference using two ideas: ON limits eligible matches.

00:01:17.242 --> 00:01:19.760
WHERE filters the final joined rows.
